2026-07-31 PR #32 (1435 -> 1435, no change -- this entry probes DATA-MISSING, it does not regenerate known-fail.tsv) [ephe: 8 files matching Tests/conformance/required-ephemeris-files.tsv, restored]: Phase 6 DATA-MISSING probe. Widened the external/swisseph sparse-checkout to the 150 era .se1 files (seas_*/semo_*/sepl_*, 28 each, plus the seasm*/semom*/seplm* BCE forms, 22 each) and ephe/sat/ (33 files), widened required-ephemeris-files.tsv to match (153 entries: the 150 era files, sefstars.txt, seorbel.txt, sat/), and ran the full 12,757-iteration corpus with SWISSEPH_CONFORMANCE_INCLUDE_JPL=1, SWISSEPH_CONFORMANCE_JPL_FILE pointed at a locally-verified de431.eph (2,788,676,624 bytes, MD5 fad0f432ae18c330f9e14915fbf8960a, kept outside the repo per EphemerisFileResolver's basename redirect) and SWISSEPH_CONFORMANCE_INCLUDE_MOONS=1. The 157 SEFLG_SWIEPH era rows needed a fourth, temporary lever: EphemerisFileResolver.NeedsEraFileWeDoNotShip is a hardcoded year<1200||year>=2400 check with no file-presence test and no existing opt-in, so it would have kept short-circuiting those rows to DataMissing regardless of the wider checkout. Added a SWISSEPH_CONFORMANCE_INCLUDE_ERA env-gated bypass to that one method, ran with it set, then reverted the source change (git checkout -- EphemerisFileResolver.cs) before restoring the checkout -- it is not part of this commit. Of the 713 DATA-MISSING rows, 504 pass outright once the data is present: 500 of 538 JPL rows and 4 of 18 sat rows. None of the 157 era rows pass. The 209 that still fail are all VALUE-MISMATCH, not DATA-MISSING, so none of them belong on the actionable porting queue by virtue of this run alone -- they are new findings, not confirmations of the existing known-fail.tsv rows. Worst magnitudes: three suite 7.3 rows (asteroid 433, Eros) and three suite 1.1 rows still cite a file this checkout does not ship at any tier tried here (se00433s.se1, a numbered-asteroid ephemeris, distinct from JPL/era/sat) -- a fourth data class, out of this probe's scope. Of the rest, the largest is suite 5.3 iteration 6's delta-T, 0.42% relative (0.0017 days); suite 10 testcase 5/6 (mooncross_node) reaches 0.054 days absolute at 2.9e-4 relative; the bulk of the remaining era rows (128 of 157, suite 10.7/10.8 swe_helio_cross) sit at roughly 1.2e-11 relative, a few ULP. These read as the tracked 2.08-vs-2.10.03 version gap, not a data problem -- expected, and not fixed here per this probe's scope. Restored: sparse-checkout reset to the original 14-pattern list, required-ephemeris-files.tsv and EphemerisFileResolver.cs reverted via git checkout, ephe/ back to 4.1 MB / 8 files. SHA-256 of both known-fail.tsv and required-ephemeris-files.tsv match their pre-run values exactly. Tests/SwissEphNet.Conformance.Tests passes clean on net8.0 and net10.0 against the restored narrow checkout. Aside, out of this probe's scope: merely having ephe/sat/ on disk (no env var needed) already flips 8 rows in the existing known-fail.tsv's VALUE-MISMATCH set (suite 1.1, SEFLG_CENTER_BODY iterations 92/95/97/98/101/104/119/122) to passing, since that code path resolves sat files directly rather than through the ipl/iplctr DataMissing gate -- these are not part of the 713 and known-fail.tsv was not touched.

2026-07-31 PR #32 (1435 -> 1435, no change -- this entry corrects the preceding one, it runs nothing) [ephe: 8 files matching Tests/conformance/required-ephemeris-files.tsv, unchanged]: Correction to the Phase 6 DATA-MISSING probe entry above. That entry describes the 128 suite 10.7/10.8 swe_helio_cross era rows as "roughly 1.2e-11 relative, a few ULP". The magnitude is right and the characterisation is wrong. One ULP at double precision is 2.2e-16 relative, so 1.2e-11 is about 54,000 ULP, and on a Julian Day near 2451545 it is 2.9e-5 days, roughly 2.5 seconds of absolute error in a crossing time. The suite 10 testcase 5 and 6 mooncross_node rows in the same entry, at 0.054 days absolute, are about 78 minutes. Neither is ULP-level noise and neither should be read as one.

The reason this matters beyond the wording: the eight crossing functions (swe_solcross, swe_solcross_ut, swe_mooncross, swe_mooncross_ut, swe_mooncross_node, swe_mooncross_node_ut, swe_helio_cross, swe_helio_cross_ut) have no bit-exactness coverage at all. Tools/OracleGrid/grid-analytic.tsv covers CALC, CALC_UT, HOUSES and HOUSES_ARMC; grid-files.tsv covers CALC, CALC_UT, the fixed-star family and GET_PLANET_NAME. Neither grid contains a crossing case, and neither Tools/CReference/sedump.c nor Tools/OracleDump/Program.cs can emit one. So the "every oracle row is bit-identical against MSVC-built 2.10.03 C" result, which holds at 14,220 of 14,220 and 2,024 of 2,024, says nothing whatever about these functions, and the probe above is the first measurement of any kind against them.

What the probe does not establish is whether the divergence is a port defect. The conformance corpus compares against setest/t.exp, not against the C, so a port that matched the C exactly would still fail these rows if t.exp's expected values were produced from ephemeris data of a different vintage than the .se1 files now shipped -- and the pinned tag's own HEAD commit message is "now created with DE441", so the shipped era files were regenerated at some point. Distinguishing the two requires running the same inputs through the MSVC-built 2.10.03 C with the same era files, which is what the oracle exists for and cannot currently do. Extending both grids and both drivers to the crossing functions is the prerequisite for any claim about them, and until that lands they should be treated as unverified rather than as a known 2.08-versus-2.10.03 gap.
